          %h2#idea Idea
          %p Event structures were introduced in order to abstract away from the precise ‘places’ and times at which events occur in distributed systems. The structure focuses on the events and the causal ordering between them.
          %p
            Event structures in the sense of this article are sometimes also called
            %em prime
            event structures. There are other variants, for example where the partial order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            is replaced by an
            %em enabling relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            = succeed ":" do
              %em disjunctive causes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vdash e
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \vdash e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            suffices for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            to occur. In a prime event structure, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq e
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq e
            then
            %em both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            must occur before
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            = succeed "." do
              %em conjunctive causes
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em event structure
              is a tuple
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,\leq, \mathrm{Con})
              consisting of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,{\le})
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %em events
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Con} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(E)
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %em consistent subsets
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  finite causes: for every event
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                  the set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e'\mid e'\le e\}
                  is finite;
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in E
                  then
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi e
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ e \} \in \mathrm{Con}
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathrm{Con}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \subseteq X
                  then
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \in \mathrm{Con}
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathrm{Con}
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in X
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi e
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e' \leq e
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi e
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \cup \{ e'\} \in \mathrm{Con}
            %p
          %p A restricted but simpler definition is as follows:
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em event structure with binary conflict
              is a tuple
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo ,
                      %mo #
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \#)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, {\leq})
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#
              is an irreflexive binary relation on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              = succeed "," do
                %em conflict relation
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  finite causes: for every event
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                  the set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e'\mid e'\le e\}
                  is finite;
              %li
                %p
                  hereditary conflict: if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                        %mo #
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \#  e'
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi e
                        %mo ″
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e' \leq e''
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo #
                          %mi e
                          %mo ″
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \# e''
            %p
          %p
            Event structures with binary conflict can be characterised as follows:
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo #
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \#)
              is an event structure with binary conflict, then defining
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi e
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo .
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo #
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Con} = \{ X \subseteq E \mid \forall e, e' \in X. \neg (e \# e') \}
              makes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \Con)
              an event structure.
            %p
              Conversely, given an event structure
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo ,
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \mathrm{Con})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi e
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \# = \{ (e, e') \mid \{ e, e'\} \notin \mathrm{Con} \}
              makes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo #
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \#)
              an event structure with binary conflict.
          %p That is, event structures with binary conflict correspond to event structures in which pairwise consistency implies mutual consistency.
          %p
            We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \Con)
            whenever possible. The possible states of an event structures are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em configurations
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              be an event structure. A
              %strong configuration
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \subseteq E
              which is consistent (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Con
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \Con
              ) and down-closed (if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e' \leq e
              then
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e' \in x
              %em finite
              configurations of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is denoted
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{C}(E)
            %p
          %h2#the_category_of_event_structures The category of event structures
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong map of event structures
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \leq, \Con)
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Con
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E', \leq', \Con')
              is a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : E \to E'
              such that:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  preserves configurations: if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in \mathscr{C}(E)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi E
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) \in \mathscr{C}(E')
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is locally injective: if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                        %mo ,
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e, e' \in x \in \mathscr{C}(E)
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi e
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi e
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(e) = f(e')
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo =
                          %mi e
                          %mo ′
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = e'
            %p
          %p
            There are obvious notions of identity and composition, and we get a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{ES}
            of event structures and maps.
          %p
            Intuitively, a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to E'
            expresses that all executions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            can be faithfully simulated within
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
          %p
            %em Partial
            maps of event structures are also important in the literature, for example in the event structure model of CCS. The definition is the same when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a partial function, and the condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(e) = f(e')
            means in particular that they are both defined. Winskel and Nielsen explain this definition as follows:
          %p
            %em
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : E\to E'
              between event structures expresses how behaviour in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              determines behaviour in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              implies the simultaneous occurrence of an event in
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(e) = e'
              can be understood as expressing that the event
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is a ‘’component’‘ of the event
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e'
              and, in this sense, that the occurrence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              implies the simultaneous occurrence of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              have the same image in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
              under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              then they cannot belong to the same configuration.
          %h2#event_structures_as_presheaves Event structures as presheaves 
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}
            denote the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{ES}
            consisting of finite event structures with no conflicts. Equivalently, this is the category of finite posets and (not necessarily monotone) maps that preserve down-closed subsets.
          %p Then the functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{ES} \longrightarrow PSh(\mathbf{P})
          %p
            that maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            to the presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ↦
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \mapsto \mathbf{ES}(P, E)
            is full and faithful, because the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi P
                  %mo ↪
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P} \hookrightarrow \mathbf{ES}
            is dense.
          %p
            The essential image of this functor is a subcategory of nonempty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated presheaves
            (for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            generated by families of jointly surjective maps) satisfying two additional conditions (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Representation"} Winskel
          %p
            (If we restrict the morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ES
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{ES}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}
            to maps of event structures which are also monotone (these are often called
            %em rigid
            maps) then a similar result holds, and the characterization of the essential image is a bit simpler.)
          %h2#event_structures_with_symmetry Event structures with symmetry
          %p
            Symmetry on an event structure is given by additional structure: an equivalence relation on configurations, such that equivalent configurations are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimulation"} bisimilar
            = succeed "." do
              %em open maps
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : E \to E'
              is
              %em open
              if it satisfies a path-lifting condition: writing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}
              for the subcategory of paths defined above, the condition requires that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
